WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.379
Protocol-aware telemetry adds industrial meaning to network communication.

00:00:04.379 --> 00:00:14.751
Instead of reporting only addresses and ports, it may identify device roles, sessions, reads, writes, commands, errors, discovery, or engineering activity.

00:00:14.751 --> 00:00:20.513
This additional context helps teams distinguish routine control from meaningful change.

00:00:20.513 --> 00:00:26.737
Modbus telemetry can separate common reads from writes and identify expected clients.

00:00:26.737 --> 00:00:33.421
DNP3 visibility may describe master and outstation relationships, event reporting, and control operations.

00:00:33.421 --> 00:00:40.105
OPC UA sources can expose application sessions, certificate events, security policy, and subscriptions.

00:00:40.105 --> 00:00:47.711
EtherNet/IP and PROFINET environments may include cyclic control traffic, diagnostics, discovery, configuration, and engineering functions.

00:00:47.711 --> 00:00:53.012
Expected patterns depend on the vendor ecosystem and engineered design.

00:00:53.012 --> 00:01:01.540
A new peer or unusual management operation may deserve review even when the protocol itself is approved.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.998
Protocol labels are not process meaning.

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:12.143
A write may be normal during startup, approved during maintenance, and concerning during production.

00:01:12.143 --> 00:01:16.522
Register, object, and tag semantics vary across sites.

00:01:16.522 --> 00:01:24.589
Asset role, process state, user authority, and work records provide the context telemetry cannot infer alone.

00:01:24.589 --> 00:01:28.507
Encrypted or proprietary communication can limit inspection.

00:01:28.507 --> 00:01:33.809
Do not weaken secure settings simply to collect more content.

00:01:33.809 --> 00:01:42.798
Endpoint logs, gateway records, controller change records, certificate events, and session metadata can fill part of the gap.

00:01:42.798 --> 00:01:47.638
Document parser coverage, protocol versions, unsupported operations, and confidence.

00:01:47.638 --> 00:01:53.400
Protocol-aware telemetry is valuable when it explains behavior without overstating intent.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:02:02.850
It should help an analyst ask better questions and engage the engineer or operator who can determine operational consequence.
